\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Docker:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 Docker is an open-source platform that automates the process of building, packaging, and running applications inside lightweight, portable containers, ensuring they run consistently across different environments.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Docker Images:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A read-only template that contains the application code, runtime, libraries, and dependencies. It is like a blueprint for creating containers.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Docker Containers:
\f2\b0\fs24 \
A running instance of a Docker image.\

\f1\b\fs28 Working with Docker CLI Commands:\
Docker CLI Commands with redis\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 Step 1: 
\f2\b0 Pull the redis Image\
Command:\
docker pull redis\

\f1\b Step 2:
\f2\b0  Run a Redis Container\
Command:\
docker run --name my-redis -d redis\
What It Does:\
\'95 Creates and starts a container named my-redis from the redis image.\
\'95 The -d flag runs the container in the background.\

\f1\b Step 3:
\f2\b0  Check Running Containers\
Command:\
docker ps\
What It Does:\
\'95 Lists all running containers.\

\f1\b Step 4:
\f2\b0  Access Redis\
Command:\
docker exec -it my-redis redis-cli\
Opens the Redis command-line tool (redis-cli) inside the container.\

\f1\b Example 
\f2\b0 Redis Commands:\
127.0.0.1:6379> SET name "Alice"\
OK\
127.0.0.1:6379> GET name\
"Alice"\
127.0.0.1:6379>\cf3 exit \cf0 // to come out\

\f1\b Step 5:
\f2\b0  Stop the Redis Container\
Command:\
docker stop my-redis\
What It Does:\
\'95 Stops the Redis container but doesn\'92t delete it.\

\f1\b Step 6:
\f2\b0  Restart the Redis Container\
Command:\
docker start my-redis\
What It Does:\
\'95 Restarts the stopped container.\

\f1\b Step 7:
\f2\b0  Remove the Redis Container\
Command:\
docker rm my-redis \'a0// Note before removing stop the container\
What It Does:\
\'95 Deletes the container permanently.\

\f1\b Step 8:
\f2\b0  Remove the Redis Image\
Command:\
docker rmi redis\
What It Does:\
\'95 Deletes the Redis image from your local system.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 2.Working with Docker file\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0\fs24 \cf0 A Dockerfile is a text file with instructions to create a custom Docker image.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Step 1:
\f2\b0  Set Up Your Folder\
1. Windows:\
o Create a folder like C:\\DockerProjects\\Redis.\
o Open Git Bash and navigate to the folder:\
cd /c/DockerProjects/Redis\
2. Mac/Linux:\
o Create a folder:\
mkdir ~/DockerProjects/Redis\
cd ~/DockerProjects/Redis\

\f1\b Step 2:
\f2\b0  Write the Dockerfile\
1. Inside the folder, create a file named Dockerfile (no extension).\
2. Add the following content:\

\f1\b FROM redis:latest\
CMD ["redis-server"]\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 Docker Commands (Step-by-step):\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 1. docker build -t redisnew 
\f2\b0\fs32 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 This creates (builds) a Docker image using the recipe (Dockerfile) in the current\
folder (.).\
\'95 -t redisnew: Gives the image a name/tag ("redisnew"), so you can find it easily.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 2. docker run --name myredisnew -d redisnew\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Starts a new container (mini computer) from the redisnew image.\
\'95 --name myredisnew: Names the container "myredisnew" so it\'92s easy to identify.\
\'95 -d: Runs the container in the background.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 3. docker ps\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Shows a list of containers that are running right now.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 4. docker stop myredisnew\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Stops the container named "myredisnew" (like turning off a computer).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 5. docker login\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Logs you into your Docker Hub account, so you can upload images.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 6. docker ps -a\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Shows a list of all containers, including stopped ones.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 7. docker commit 0e993d2009a1 budarajumadhurika/redis1\
// Note 0e993d2009a1 is container id of myredisnew\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Takes a snapshot (saves changes) of the container with ID 0e993d2009a1 and creates a\
new image called budarajumadhurika/redis1.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 8. docker images\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Lists all images saved on your system. // will show image of budarajumadhurika/redis1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 9. docker push budarajumadhurika/redis1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Uploads the image budarajumadhurika/redis1 to Docker Hub, so others can download\
it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 10. docker rm 0e993d2009a1 \'a0// container id of myredisnew\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Deletes the container with ID 0e993d2009a1 
\f1\b of myredisnew
\f2\b0 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 11. docker rmi budarajumadhurika/redis1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Deletes the image budarajumadhurika/redis1 from your system.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 12. docker ps -a\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
Shows all containers again to confirm changes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 13. docker logout\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Logs you out of Docker Hub.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 14. docker pull budarajumadhurika/redis1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Downloads the image budarajumadhurika/redis1 from Docker Hub.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 15. docker run --name myredis -d budarajumadhurika/redis1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Starts a new container using the image budarajumadhurika/redis1.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 16. docker exec -it myredis redis-cli\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Opens the Redis command-line interface (like a terminal) inside the running container\
myredis.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 17. SET name "Abcdef"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Saves a key-value pair in Redis (key = name, value = Abcdef).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 18. GET name\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Retrieves the value of the key name from Redis (it will return "Abcdef").\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 19. exit\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Exits the Redis CLI.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 20. docker ps -a\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Shows all containers again to check their status.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 21. docker stop myredis\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Stops the container myredis.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 22. docker rm 50a6e4a9c326\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Deletes the container with ID 50a6e4a9c326.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 23. docker images\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Lists all images again to confirm which ones remain.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 24. docker rmi budarajumadhurika/redis1\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 What it does:\
\'95 Deletes the image budarajumadhurika/redis1 again.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Step 3: Remove Login Credentials (Optional)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa160\partightenfactor0

\f2\b0 \cf0 If you no longer need to be logged in, you can log out:\
docker logout\
What It Does:\
\'95 Logs you out from Docker Hub and removes your stored credentials.\
\
